          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#traditional Traditional
          %p
            Multisymplectic geometry is a generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+systems"} mechanical systems
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            is generalized from a closed 2-form to a closed
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            – the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+form"} n-plectic form
          %p
            It is closely related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Donder-Weyl+formalism"} de Donder-Weyl formalism
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            it is meant to provide a refinement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            which is well-adapted to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h3#from_the_pov
            From the
            = succeed "POV" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            We comment a bit on how to, presumably, think of multisymplectic geometry from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#extendedphasespace"} Extended phase space
          .standout
            %p
              Multisymplectic geometry is (or should be) to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
              as
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/FQFT#Extended"} extended quantum field theory
              is to non-extended
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %p
              in the multisymplectic
              %strong extended phase space
              of an
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} field theory
              a state is not just a point, but an
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} n-plectic geometry
          %p
            %strong Multisymplectic geometry
            is a generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            to cases where the symplectic 2-form is generalized to a higher degree
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
          %p
            In as far as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            encodes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            symplectic form.
          %p
            In this application to physics, multisymplectic geometry is also known as the
            %strong covariant symplectic approach
            to field theory (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/physics/pdf/9801/9801019v2.pdf#page=18"} section 2 here
          %p
            The idea is that under a suitable fiber integration multisymplectic geometry becomes ordinary symplectic form on the ordinary phase space of the theory, similar to, and in fact as a special case of, how for instance a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            with a 2-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            may arise by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
            from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            down on the original space, with a 3-form class.
          %p
            By effectively undoing this implicit transgression in the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %em covariant formulation
            means that spacelike and timelike directions on a given space-time be treated on equal footing.
          %h2#extendedphasespace Extended phase spaces in covariant field theory
          %p
            We discuss here the refinement in multisymplectic geometry of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+spaces"} covariant phase spaces
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            from (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"}> pre
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            of initial value data in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            to multisymplectic manifolds of local initial value data.
          %p
            Recall that an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            whose points correspond to the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            of the system. The
            %strong extended phase space
            of an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %p a point in the field theory’s parameter space – an “event”;
            %li
              %p a state of the theory “at that event”.
          %p
            So
            %strong
              extended phase spaces
              %em localizes
              the information about states
            \: a point in here encodes not just the entire state of the system, but remembers explicitly what that state is like over any point in parameter space.
          %h3#covariant_configuration_bundle Covariant configuration bundle
          %p
            Consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            over a
            %strong parameter space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            will be one fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            on which we want to understand the (classical) field theory.
          %p
            We assume that a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field configuration
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \Sigma \to E
            of some prescribed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \Sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For instance an
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              is one whose field configurations on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              are given by maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi : \Sigma \longrightarrow X
            %p
              into some prescribed
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = \Sigma \times X
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  trivial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Beware of the standard source of confusion here when correlating this formalism with actual physics: the physical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              that we inhabit may be given either by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              or by
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  in the description of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                  of objects propagating
                  %em in
                  our physical
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
                  exerted by fixed
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
                  (such as electrons propagating in our particle accelerator, subject to the electromagnetic field in the accelerator tube), physical spacetime is identified with target space
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  is the
                  %strong worldvolume
                  of the object that propagates through
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  %em field configurations
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  are really the maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
                  that determine how the object sits in spacetime.
              %li
                %p
                  in quantum mechanics of fields on spacetime, such as the quantized electromagnetic field in a laser, it is
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  which represents physical spacetime, and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  is some abstract space, for instance a smooth version of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
                  encodes a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
                  that encodes a configuration of the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong configuration space
              of the system is the space of all field configurations, hence the space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_\Sigma(E)
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            example this is some incarnation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,X]
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Beware that in low dimensions one often distinguishes between the space of
              %em configurations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
              and that of
              %em trajectories
              or
              %em histories
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \times \mathbb{R} \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = *
              where for a particle propagating on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [*,X] \simeq X
              are the possible configurations of the particle at a given parameter times, while maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo *
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [* \times \mathbb{R}, X] = [\mathbb{R}, X]
              are the trajectories. But for the higher dimensional and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended
              field theories under discussion here, this distinction becomes a bit obsolete and trajectories become just a special case of configurations.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              In the
              %strong non-covariant
              approach one would try to consider the a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
              of the configuration space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(E)
              as
              %em phase space
              \. Contrary to that, in the
              %strong covariant approach
              one considers the much smaller space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              instead. This is then called the
              %strong covariant configuration space
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %strong covariant configuration bundle
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E \to \Sigma
              for the first order
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
              of the configuration space bundle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \Sigma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in \Sigma
              are equivalence classses of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germs"} germs
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h3#covariant_phase_space Covariant phase space
          #DualFirstJetBundle.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \Sigma
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim(\Sigma)  = n+1
              %strong affine dual first jet bundle
              (or often just
              %strong dual first jet bundle
              for short)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J_1 E)^\ast \to \Sigma
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in E_s
              is the set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+maps"} affine maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi J
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    J_e^1 E \longrightarrow \Lambda_s^{n+1} \Sigma
            %p
              from the first
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jets
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              to the degree-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          #ExtendedCovariantPhaseSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \Sigma
              %strong extended covariant phase space
              is the multisymplectic manifold
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  whose underlying manifold is the dual first jet bundle, def.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DualFirstJetBundle"}
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi J
                          %mn 1
                        %mi E
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo →
                        %mi E
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
