                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to X
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                parameter space:
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo =
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong worldline
            %li
              %p
                target space:
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                –
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong spacetime
            %li
              %p
                configuration bundle:
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E \to \mathbb{R}) = (\mathbb{R}\times X \to \mathbb{R})
            %li
              %p
                jet bundle:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi J
                        %mn 1
                      %mi E
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E = \mathbb{R} \times T X
                \.
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset E = \mathbb{R} \times X
            a local patch with coordinate functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t, q^i\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E
            written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi v
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t,q^i, v^i\}
          %p
            Here a collection of vaues
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi q
                    %mn 0
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q^i_0)
            is a
            %strong position
            of the particle and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v^i_0)
            is a
            %strong velocity
            of the particle. Notice that in this covariant approach these are not positions and velocities “at a given time”. Rather, a point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E
            specified a parameter time and a corresponding position and velocity.
          %p ….
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
            be a local patch of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with canonical coordinates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}
          %p The canonical 2-form on the extended phase space in this case is traditionally locally written as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega|_U = \mathbf{d} \alpha|_U = \mathbf{d}( p_i \wedge \mathbf{d} q^i + H \wedge d t )
                  \\,.
          %p … blah-blah-blah…
          %h4#electromagnetism Electromagnetism
          %ul
            %li
              parameter space:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is spacetime;
          %p
            A field configuration of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} 1-form
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %ul
            %li
              configuration bundle:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* \Sigma
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %h4#bosonic_string_propagating_on_a_manifold Bosonic string propagating on a manifold
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                parameter space:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                some 2-dimensional manifold – the
                %strong worldsheet
                – for instance
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = S^1 \times \mathbb{R}
                models a closed string propagating without interaction.
            %li
              %p
                target space.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                spacetime;
            %li
              %p
                covariant configuration bundle
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo =
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = \Sigma \times X
          %p
            We will work out the covariant Hamiltonian formalism (also known as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Donder-Weyl+formalism"} de Donder-Weyl formalism
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Helein02"} Hélein 02
          %p
            For simplicity we will only consider the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is the cylinder
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\times S^1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \Sigma \to X
            which is a critical point of the area subject to certain boundary conditions.
          %p
            Equivalently, by exploiting symmetries in the variational problem, one can describe solutions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            by equipping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times S^{1}
            with its standard Minkowski metric and then solving the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+1
            dimensional field theory specified by the Lagrangian density
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi ab
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi j
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2} g^{ij}\eta_{ab} \frac{\partial
                  \\phi^{a}}{\partial q^i}\frac{\partial \phi^{b}}{\partial q^j}.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i = 0,1)
            are standard coordinates on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times S^1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} diag
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=\mathrm{diag}(1,-1)
            is the Minkowski metric on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^a
            are the coordinates of the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d-1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} diag
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta =
                  \\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,\cdots,-1)
            is the Minkowski metric on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d-1}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g^{ij}\partial_{i} \partial_{j} \phi^a =0.
          %p
            The space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E=\Sigma \times X
            can be thought of as a trivial bundle over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \Sigma \to X
            is a smooth section of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q,u)\in E
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mi a
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left(q^i,u^a \right)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E \to E
            be the first jet bundle of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E
            as a vector bundle whose fiber over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q,u)\in E
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi T
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi u
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*_q \Sigma \otimes T_u X
            = succeed "The" do
              %br/
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi ab
                  %msubsup
                    %mi u
                    %mi i
                    %mi a
                  %msubsup
                    %mi u
                    %mi j
                    %mi b
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2} g^{ij}\eta_{ab}u^{a}_{i}u^{b}_{j},
          %p
            which depends in this example only on the fiber coordinates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi u
                      %mi i
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^a_{i}
          %p
            From the Lagrangian
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L} : J^{1}E \to \mathbb{R}
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Donder-Weyl+Hamiltonian"} de Donder-Weyl Hamiltonian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H} : T \Sigma \otimes T^*X \to \mathbb{R}
            can be constructed via a Legendre transform. It is given as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msubsup
                    %mi u
                    %mi i
                    %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi η
                    %mi ab
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi b
                    %mi j
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{H}= p^{i}_{a}u^{a}_{i}- \mathcal{L} =\frac{1}{2} \eta^{ab}g_{ij}p_{a}^{i}p_{b}^{j},
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi u
                    %mi i
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^a_{i}
            are defined implicitly by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ∂
                    %msubsup
                      %mi u
                      %mi i
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_a^{i}=\partial \mathcal{L} / \partial u^{a}_{i}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_a^{i}
            are coordinates on the fiber
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi T
                      %mi u
                      %mo *
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi q
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{*}_{u}X \otimes T_{q}\Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            differs from the standard (non-covariant) Hamiltonian density for a field theory:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                  %msubsup
                    %mi u
                    %mn 0
                    %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p^{0}_{a}u^{a}_{0} - \mathcal{L}.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            be a section of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            be a smooth section of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \Sigma \otimes T^*X
            restricted to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(\Sigma)
            with fiber coordinates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{a}^{i}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is a solution of the Euler-Lagrange equations if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            satisfy the following system of equations:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi ℋ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %msup
                            %mi u
                            %mi a
                      %mo |
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\partial \pi^{i}_{a}}{\partial q^i} =
                  \-  \left.\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial
                  u^{a}} \right \vert_{u=\phi,p=\pi}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi ℋ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %msubsup
                            %mi p
                            %mi a
                            %mi i
                      %mo |
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mi π
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\partial \phi^{a}}{\partial q^i} = \left.\frac{\partial \mathcal{H}}{\partial
                  p^i_{a}} \right \vert_{u=\phi,p=\pi}.
          %p This system of equations is a generalization of Hamilton’s equations for the point particle.
          %p
            As explained above, the covariant phase space for the bosonic string is the dual jet bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J^1 E)^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            whose exterior derivative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = d \alpha
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+form"} multisymplectic 3-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi T
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J^1 E)^* \cong  T \Sigma \otimes T^*X \times \mathbb{R} ,
          %p
            a point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J^1 E)^{*}
            gets coordinates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi u
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q^i,u^a,p^{i}_{a},e)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %msup
                    %mi dq
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi dq
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi du
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi du
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 0
                    %mo )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha= e dq^{0} \wedge dq^{1} +
                  \\left(p_{a}^{0} du^{a} \wedge dq^{1} -
                  p_{a}^{1} du^{a} \wedge dq^{0} \right) .
          %p
            The multisymplectic structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J^1 E)^*
            is thus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi de
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi dq
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi dq
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi dp
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi du
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi dp
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi du
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 0
                    %mo )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega =
                  de \wedge dq^0 \wedge dq^{1} +
                  \\left(dp_a^0 \wedge du^a \wedge dq^{1} -
                  dp_a^1 \wedge du^a \wedge dq^{0} \right) .
          %p
            So, the variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            may be considered as canonically conjugate to the area form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dq
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dq^{0} \wedge dq^{1}
          %p
            As before, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            be a section of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            be a smooth section of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \Sigma \otimes T^*X
            restricted to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(\Sigma)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset (J^1 E)^*
            with coordinates:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (q^i,\phi^{a}(q^j),\pi_{a}^{i}(q^j),-\mathcal{H}).
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is constructed from
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            and from the constraint
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e + \mathcal{H}=0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v=v_{0} \wedge v_{1}
            can be defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msubsup
                        %mi p
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v_{0} =\frac{\partial}{\partial q^{0}} +
                  \\frac{\partial \phi^a}{\partial q^{0}}\frac{\partial}{\partial u^a} +
                  \\frac{\partial \pi_{a}^{i}}{\partial q^{0}}
                  \\frac{\partial}{\partial p_{a}^{i}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi u
                        %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msubsup
                        %mi p
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v_{1} = \frac{\partial}{\partial q^{1}} +
                  \\frac{\partial \phi^a}{\partial q^{1}}
                  \\frac{\partial}{\partial u^a} +
                  \\frac{\partial \pi_{a}^{i}}{\partial q^{1}}
                  \\frac{\partial}{\partial p_{a}^{i}}.
          %p
            Explicit computation reveals that the submanifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is generated by solutions to Hamilton’s equations if and only if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega(v_{0},v_{1},\cdot)=0.
          %h2#hamiltonian_dimensional_flow
            Hamiltonian
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+n-vector+field"} Hamiltonian n-vector field
          %h2#relation_to_symplectic_manifolds
            Relation to
            = succeed "-symplectic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p There is also the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-symplectic+manifold"}> n-symplectic manifold
              s.
          %p Which is different, but related…
          %p ….blah-blah….
          %h2#SurveyDevelopments Survey of developments in the field
          %p
            There is in this sense a covariant form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendre+transformation"} Legendre transformation
            which associates to every field variable as many conjugated momenta – the multimomenta – as there are space-time dimensions. These, together with the field variables, those of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+form"} multisymplectic form
          %p
            Among the achievements of the multisymplectic approach is a geometric formulation of the relation of infinitesimal symmetries and covariantly conserved quantities (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether%27s+theorem"}> Noether's theorem
            ), see [4] for a recent review, and [5,6] for a clarification of the improvement techniques (“Belinfante-Rosenfeld formula”) of the energy-momentum tensor in classical field theory.
          %p Multisymplectic geometry also provides convenient sets of variational integrators for the numerical study of partial differential equations [7].
          %p
            Since in multisymplectic geometry, the symplectic 2-form of classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            is replaced by a closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            of higher tensor degree, multivector fields and differential forms have their natural appearance. (See [8] for an interpretation of multivector fields as describing solutions to field equations infinitesimally.) Multivector fields form a
            %span.newWikiWord
              graded Lie algebra
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graded+Lie+algebra"} ?
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schouten+bracket"} Schouten bracket
            (see [9] for a review and unified viewpoint). Using the multisymplectic
            = succeed "-form," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %p
            A remarkable fact is that in order to establish a Jacobi identity, the multisymplectic form has to have a potential, a condition that is not seen in symplectic geometry. Further, the admissible differential forms, the Poisson forms, are subject to the rather strong restrictions on their dependence on the multimomentum variables [11]. In particular,
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
          %p
            Not much is known about the interpretation of Poisson forms of form degree between zero and n-1. However, as
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p
            The Hamiltonian, infinite dimensional formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            requires the choice of a spacelike hypersurface (“Cauchy surface”) [12] which manifestly breaks the general covariance of the theory at hand. For
            = succeed "-forms," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %em à la
            Dirac [15,16] can be performed [17]. Again, the necessary breaking of general covariance raises the need for an alternative formulation of all this [18]; first attempts have been made to carry out a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marsden-Weinstein+reduction"} Marsden-Weinstein reduction
            [19] for multisymplectic manifolds with symmetries [20]. However, not very much is known about how to quantize a multisymplectic geometry, see [21] for an approach using a path integral.
          %p This discussion so far concerns field theories of first order, i.e. where the Lagrangian depends on the fields and their first derivatives. Higher order theories can be reduced to first order ones for the price of introducing auxiliary fields. A direct treatment would involve higher order jet bundles [22]. A definition of the covariant Legendre transform and the multiphase space has been given for this case [3].
          %h2#references References
          %h3#on_classical_multisymplectic_geometry On classical multisymplectic geometry
          %p A comprehensive source on covariant field theory with plenty of further references is
          %ul
            %li
              Mark J. Gotay, James Isenberg,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jerrold+Marsden"} Jerrold Marsden
              %em Momentum maps and classical relativistic fields. Part I: covariant field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/physics/9801019"}> arXiv:physics/9801019
              )
          %p
            Much of the material in the
            %a{:href => "#extendedphasespace"} section on covariant field theory
            is based on this.
          %p Further discussions:
          %ul
            %li#Helein02
              %p
